{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The maximum-likelihood estimator (MLE) is the value of parameter $\\theta$ that maximizes the likelihood function $\\mathcal{L}(\\theta) = \\prod_{i=1}^{n} p(x_i | \\theta)$ where $x_i$ are i.i.d. observations from a distribution with unknown parameter $\\theta$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The lecture states: 'So we have, like, the usual knowledge of the maximum likelihood estimator, as we've written in the chat. This is the first way that you have thought that you could learn on statistics courses.' This indicates that the MLE is the standard approach for estimating $\\theta$ based on i.i.d. observations."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The likelihood function is a product of probabilities $p(x_i | \\theta)$ for i.i.d. observations $x_i$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The lecture states: 'So we simply compute maximum likelihood estimation. So we're trying to maximize so-called likelihood function. And the likelihood function, in this case, is simply a product of those distributions where we substitute our observations in our density function.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The likelihood function is treated as a function of $\\theta$ (not the observations $x_i$), which are fixed.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The lecture states: 'So, pay attention that we treat this function not as a function of x's, because x's are null, but as a function of theta.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The likelihood function is not a probability distribution or density function.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The lecture states: 'And as a function of theta, this is not a distribution, this is not a probability density function, this is a logarithmic function.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Taking the logarithm of the likelihood function converts the product into a sum, which is easier to maximize.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The lecture states: 'So we simply take the product of those densities. Of course, in practice, the first thing we should do is to switch to a log likelihood in order to be able to maximize the sum of functions rather than [the product].'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The log-likelihood function is defined as $\\ell(\\theta) = \\log \\mathcal{L}(\\theta) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\log p(x_i | \\theta)$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The lecture states: 'So we simply take the product of those densities. Of course, in practice, the first thing we should do is to switch to a log likelihood in order to be able to maximize the sum of functions rather than [the product].'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The likelihood function is a logarithmic function when treated as a function of $\\theta$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The lecture states: 'And as a function of theta, this is not a distribution, this is not a probability density function, this is a logarithmic function.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The lecture emphasizes that switching to log likelihood is a practical step to handle the maximization problem.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The lecture states: 'Of course, in practice, the first thing we should do is to switch to a log likelihood in order to be able to maximize the sum of functions rather than [the product].'"
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "The lecture does not explicitly state the mathematical definition of the log-likelihood function as a sum of logs, but the context implies it. The timestamped quote provides the rationale for using log-likelihood but does not give the exact formula."
}
